  "response": "The dispute over the fate of the core of SN 1987A begins with the progenitor itself. Sanduleak \u221269 202 was a red supergiant at the time of its explosion, and that explosion occurred well before any of the signals later analysed on Earth were gathered. Underground detectors Kamiokande-II, IMB and Baksan recorded a burst of about two dozen neutrinos from the supernova, which shows that the stellar core collapsed into a hot proto-neutron star. The timing of the recorded burst shows that the detected neutrinos arrived over a span of roughly ten seconds; that is, the neutrino signal from the collapse lasted on the order of a dozen seconds.\n\nBecause the collapse had produced a hot proto-neutron star rather than an immediate void, the remnant's later history depended entirely on how much material rained back onto that newborn surface in the hours that followed. Continued fallback accretion onto the proto-neutron star could have driven it over the maximum mass and produced a black hole, an outcome that cannot be squared with the ALMA observations reported in 2019, which revealed a compact hot dust blob offset from the explosion site.\n\nDiscriminating between an obscured neutron star and a black hole therefore meant hunting for the signature of a cooling, magnetised surface across every waveband the remnant allows, since the centre has never been resolved as a point source in visible light. Hubble Space Telescope imaging placed tight upper limits on the optical luminosity of any central compact object. Hard X-ray spectra obtained with Chandra and NuSTAR are consistent with emission from a pulsar wind nebula embedded in the ejecta.\n\nTwo further strands of reasoning closed the argument. The reason the centre appears so feeble at optical wavelengths, and hence the reason the Hubble limits come out as tight as they do, lies in the material that has condensed between the compact object and the observer. The hard X-ray spectra, for their part, serve as evidence about what must be illuminating the innermost gas. Cold dust condensing in the inner ejecta absorbs optical and ultraviolet radiation from the remnant's centre. The ionization state of the central gas requires a persistent source of ultraviolet and X-ray photons. The JWST detection of the central emission lines was made with the NIRCam imaging camera.\n\nThe absorbing material in the inner ejecta amounts, in other words, to a dense clump of dust and ejecta that enshrouds the compact object and blocks its direct light. At least one of two circumstances must accordingly hold of the centre, and perhaps both together: that shroud, and an intrinsically faint thermal surface emission of the compact object. On this reading the missing object is a neutron star hidden from view rather than a black hole, and the question was settled not by a direct image but by the energy budget of the gas that the object continues to ionize.",
